Chapter: AppRunnerFrame
  cites UI chapter AppRunner
  cites UI chapter Orchestrator
  cites UI chapter Overlay
  cites UI chapter InputSource
  cites UI chapter Widget
  cites UI chapter Theme

 The frame loop GuiOS actually runs on. `GuiShell` is the only consumer
 and it reaches nine of this chapter's definitions: `bare-app-new` once at
 boot, then `bare-app-tick`, `bare-app-input`, `bare-app-set-root` at five
 sites, `bare-app-set-quit` on Escape, and the `ba-quit` and `ba-state`
 field reads. Everything drawn on a GuiOS screen passes through that loop.

 The menu helpers are a different case and are asked here too. Nothing in
 the tree calls `bare-app-open-menu`, `bare-app-close-menu`,
 `bare-app-close-all-menus`, `bare-app-has-menu`, `bare-app-has-any-menu`
 or `bare-menu-width` -- not GuiShell, not any app. They are latent, so
 these lines pin them before a caller arrives rather than after.

 The two menu queries are asked together on purpose. `bare-app-has-any-menu`
 reads `os-count` and `bare-app-has-menu` walks the overlay list, so they
 are two different answers to one question and can disagree. This chapter
 prints both beside the raw count so a disagreement is visible rather than
 inferred.

 The GuiOS golden frames are not coverage for any of this. They compare
 pixels of a painted desktop; a frame counter that never advances, a
 `set-root` that discards state, and a menu that cannot be closed all paint
 an identical first frame.

 Measured 2026-07-28 against seed 104567BE: every reading below is
 CORRECT. The chapter is pinned rather than repaired. Three sabotages were
 fired to establish that these lines can fail, each on exactly its own
 rows:

 - `bare-app-tick-step` carrying `ba.ba-state` instead of the ticked
   `st2` collapses the frame line to 0 0 0 0 and takes the three rows
   downstream of it that read a frame. The dimensions, the triangle
   budget and all four menu rows hold.
 - `bare-app-set-root` writing `app-frame = 0` moves the set-root row and
   nothing else at all.
 - `overlay-dismiss` keeping the old `os-count` after filtering moves the
   close row and the agreement row to `one=2/1`, with the two-menu and
   empty states holding.

 The menu-width row needs no sabotage: it asks three lists whose answers
 must differ from each other, so a width that ignored its items could not
 produce them.

 We say:

Section: Reading

  payload : WidgetNode -> Text
  payload (w) = when w.wn-kind
    is WkLabel (t) -> "[" & t & "]"
    is WkButton (t) -> "<" & t & ">"
    is WkGauge (v) (m) -> "gauge"
    is WkInput (t) (c) -> "input(" & t & ")"
    is WkSeparator -> "sep"
    is WkPanel -> "panel"
    is WkCustom (tag) -> "custom(" & tag & ")"

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

  root-of : BareApp -> Text
  root-of (ba) = payload ((ba.ba-state).app-root)

  frame-of : BareApp -> Integer
  frame-of (ba) = bare-app-frame ba

  count-of : BareApp -> Integer
  count-of (ba) = (((ba.ba-state).app-overlays).os-count)

Section: The apps under test

  root-a : WidgetNode
  root-a = widget-label "r1" "Alpha"

  root-b : WidgetNode
  root-b = widget-label "r2" "Beta"

  app0 : BareApp
  app0 = bare-app-new root-a theme-terminal 640 480 4096

 A neutral input reports no movement and no key, which is the common case
 in the shell: most frames arrive with nothing pressed.

  quiet : RawInput
  quiet = raw-input-new 640 480

Section: Construction

  line-new : Text =
    let f = show (frame-of app0)
    in let q = yn (app0.ba-quit)
    in let w = show (app0.ba-width)
    in let h = show (app0.ba-height)
    in let t = show (app0.ba-max-tris)
    in "new: frame=" & f & " quit=" & q & " w=" & w & " h=" & h & " tris=" & t & " root=" & root-of app0

Section: The frame counter

 Asked three times because a counter that always answers the same thing
 looks exactly like one that works, and asked on a QUIET input because
 that is the frame the shell sees most often. A loop that only advances
 when something is pressed would still pass a single-tick reading.

  tick1 : BareApp
  tick1 = bare-app-tick-step app0 quiet

  tick2 : BareApp
  tick2 = bare-app-tick-step tick1 quiet

  tick3 : BareApp
  tick3 = bare-app-tick-step tick2 quiet

  line-frames : Text =
    let a = show (frame-of app0)
    in let b = show (frame-of tick1)
    in let c = show (frame-of tick2)
    in let d = show (frame-of tick3)
    in "frames: start=" & a & " t1=" & b & " t2=" & c & " t3=" & d

 The dimensions and the triangle budget are carried through the tick by
 hand, field by field, so they are the rows a mistake in that copy would
 move. They are the control for every reading below.

  line-tick-carries : Text =
    let w = show (tick3.ba-width)
    in let h = show (tick3.ba-height)
    in let t = show (tick3.ba-max-tris)
    in let q = yn (tick3.ba-quit)
    in let r = root-of tick3
    in "tick carries: w=" & w & " h=" & h & " tris=" & t & " quit=" & q & " root=" & r

Section: Setting the root

 `shell-set-view`, `shell-rebuild`, `shell-nav-up`, `shell-nav-down` and
 `shell-sidebar-click` all rebuild the whole tree and hand it here, every
 time the user moves. A `set-root` that reset the frame or dropped the
 quit flag would strand the shell on frame zero forever.

  after-root : BareApp
  after-root = bare-app-set-root tick3 root-b

  line-set-root : Text =
    let r = root-of after-root
    in let f = show (frame-of after-root)
    in let q = yn (after-root.ba-quit)
    in let w = show (after-root.ba-width)
    in "set-root: root=" & r & " frame=" & f & " quit=" & q & " w=" & w

Section: Setting quit

  after-quit : BareApp
  after-quit = bare-app-set-quit tick3

  line-set-quit : Text =
    let q = yn (after-quit.ba-quit)
    in let r = root-of after-quit
    in let f = show (frame-of after-quit)
    in "set-quit: quit=" & q & " root=" & r & " frame=" & f

Section: What the caller still holding the app sees

 `shell-set-view` passes `ss.ss-app` in and stores the result in a fresh
 ShellState, so the original is discarded and an in-place write would be
 invisible there. It is read AFTER the call here, not snapshotted before,
 because a Text bound before the call records what the app HAD been and
 could never disagree with itself.

  line-alias : Text =
    let original = root-of tick3
    in let f = show (frame-of tick3)
    in "alias: original-after=" & original & " frame-after=" & f

Section: The menu helpers

 Latent. Two menus are opened so that closing one by id can be told apart
 from closing everything, which a single menu cannot express.

  menu-items : List Text
  menu-items = ["Open", "Save", "Quit"]

  m1 : BareApp
  m1 = bare-app-open-menu app0 "file" 10 20 menu-items

  m2 : BareApp
  m2 = bare-app-open-menu m1 "edit" 60 20 menu-items

  m-closed-one : BareApp
  m-closed-one = bare-app-close-menu m2 "file"

  m-closed-all : BareApp
  m-closed-all = bare-app-close-all-menus m2

  line-menu-open : Text =
    let c1 = show (count-of m1)
    in let c2 = show (count-of m2)
    in let f = yn (bare-app-has-menu m2 "file")
    in let e = yn (bare-app-has-menu m2 "edit")
    in let n = yn (bare-app-has-menu m2 "none")
    in "menu open: count1=" & c1 & " count2=" & c2 & " file=" & f & " edit=" & e & " absent=" & n

  line-menu-close : Text =
    let c = show (count-of m-closed-one)
    in let f = yn (bare-app-has-menu m-closed-one "file")
    in let e = yn (bare-app-has-menu m-closed-one "edit")
    in let a = show (count-of m-closed-all)
    in let any = yn (bare-app-has-any-menu m-closed-all)
    in "menu close: one=" & c & " file=" & f & " edit=" & e & " all=" & a & " any=" & any

 The stored count against the entries actually locatable by walking. These
 are two different derivations of one number: `os-count` is a field the
 stack maintains, and `bare-app-has-menu` reaches each entry through
 `overlay-find`. An entry removed without the count following, or a count
 decremented without the entry going, shows up here and nowhere else.

 Asking `bare-app-has-any-menu` against `count-of` instead would be a line
 that cannot fail, because both read `os-count`. That was the first draft
 of this line. An identity between two callers of one field tests the
 callers, not the field.

  findable : BareApp -> Integer
  findable (ba) =
    let f = if bare-app-has-menu ba "file" then 1 else 0
    in let e = if bare-app-has-menu ba "edit" then 1 else 0
    in f + e

  line-menu-agree : Text =
    let c2 = show (count-of m2)
    in let f2 = show (findable m2)
    in let c1 = show (count-of m-closed-one)
    in let f1 = show (findable m-closed-one)
    in let c0 = show (count-of m-closed-all)
    in let f0 = show (findable m-closed-all)
    in "menu agree: two=" & c2 & "/" & f2 & " one=" & c1 & "/" & f1 & " none=" & c0 & "/" & f0

 Three items of four characters give a width of 4*6+16, and a longer item
 must widen it. A width that ignored its items would answer the same for
 both.

  line-menu-width : Text =
    let a = show (bare-menu-width menu-items 0 3 0)
    in let b = show (bare-menu-width ["A", "Longer"] 0 2 0)
    in let c = show (bare-menu-width [] 0 0 0)
    in "menu width: three=" & a & " longer=" & b & " empty=" & c

Section: Body

  report-a : Text =
    line-new & "\n" & line-frames & "\n" & line-tick-carries

  report-b : Text =
    line-set-root & "\n" & line-set-quit & "\n" & line-alias

  report-c : Text =
    line-menu-open & "\n" & line-menu-close & "\n" & line-menu-agree & "\n" & line-menu-width

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni report-a
    print-line-uni report-b
    print-line-uni report-c
  end
